@h1 Multi-file documents & references h1@

@@@code reference ./path.wit code@@@ pulls another file into the document's
reference graph. Within that graph, @strong definitions and data are global and
hoisted strong@ — one flat namespace, visible regardless of order. This is how
you split a thesis, report, or manual into chapter files that share one schema.

@h2 The reference directive h2@

Put @@@code reference ./chapter.wit code@@@ on its own line. The referenced
file's defs and data become available to the whole document:

@@@pre
~ master.wit
reference ./one.wit

The keeper is @keeper.name.

~ one.wit
#keeper: { name - Ada }
pre@@@

That renders @code The keeper is Ada. code@. Path forms are
same-directory @@@code ./one.wit code@@@,
a child directory @@@code ./sub/x.wit code@@@ (nested-subdir), and a parent
@@@code ../shared.wit code@@@ (parent-relative).

@h2 One flat namespace, hoisted h2@

Multiple references form @strong one namespace strong@, and visibility is @strong transitive strong@ — a master
that references A which references B can see B's definitions
(transitive-references). Because everything is hoisted, the @strong position of
the reference line is immaterial strong@: a use that appears @em before em@ the
@@@code reference code@@@ still resolves (forward-then-back).

@@@pre
~ master.wit
The keeper is @keeper.name.

reference ./one.wit
pre@@@

Still renders @code The keeper is Ada. code@.

@h2 Shared schemas and diamonds h2@

Two chapters may both reference a shared schema file; the re-convergence (a
diamond) resolves once. This is the idiom for a common
set of card, citation, and figure templates used across every chapter.

@h2 Self, circular, and missing h2@

A self-reference is a no-op via the already-visited set;
a circular pair is handled by cycle detection (circular-references); a missing
path is a resolver error (missing-file).

@aside(class note) 💡 The @code 14-composition code@ fixtures snapshot the @em parse em@ stage — they record the @@@code reference code@@@ node without flattening. Circular and missing-file handling are @strong resolver-stage strong@ behaviours, seen at build time, not parse errors. aside@

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Expecting a @strong conditional strong@ reference — wrapping
@@@code reference code@@@ in an @@@code (if) code@@@ is not supported. To include
different subsets, choose a different build root (see Faceted content). li@
@li Expecting a mid-file @@@code reference code@@@ to scope-limit — it is global
and hoisted. li@
@li Relative-path confusion — mind @@@code ./ code@@@, @@@code ../ code@@@, and
@@@code ./sub/ code@@@. li@
ul@

